course: euphemisms
lesson: 36
title: Soft words for hard things: stupidity and madness
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Calling someone stupid or mad to their face is rude and hurts, so people wrap the idea in softer or funnier words.
explain: Some of these words are kind: a teacher who says a boy is "more practical than academic" means school work is hard for him, but he is good with his hands.
explain: Others are jokes that paint a picture: "a screw loose" makes a person sound like a machine with one part wobbling, and "thick as two short planks" compares a slow thinker to pieces of wood.
explain: Many old words for madness are jokes, and real mental illness is no joke, so a kind person does not use them about someone who is truly unwell.
explain: To understand these phrases, ask: is the speaker being gentle, polite, or making fun?
fact: "A bit dim" means rather stupid; it is everyday British English and sounds softer than "stupid".
fact: "Thick as two short planks" means very stupid; it is a British joke.
fact: "Intellectually challenged" is a jokey way of saying stupid, copying the style of official polite words.
fact: "An ID-ten-T error" means a mistake the user made himself because he was being an idiot: written down, ID10T spells "idiot"; it is computer jokers' talk.
fact: "Not the most academic" means not clever at school work; it is a polite way to put it.
fact: "More practical than academic" means not good at school or books, but often good at doing things with the hands.
fact: "Not firing on all cylinders" means not thinking clearly, like an engine running badly; often said about someone tired or ill.
fact: "Has a screw loose" means is a bit mad or acts strangely.
fact: "Off one's rocker" means mad; it is slang.
fact: "Round the bend" means mad or very annoyed; "you're driving me round the bend" means you are making me crazy with annoyance. It is British.
fact: "Round the twist" means mad, just like "round the bend"; it is British.
fact: "Away with the fairies" means daydreaming, not paying attention, or a bit mad; it is British, and it is usually said gently and kindly about a dreamy person.
fact: "Off with the pixies" means daydreaming or not quite sane; it is said in New Zealand and Australia.
fact: "Not all there" means not quite sane (sane means thinking in a normal, clear way, not mad), or a bit slow in the head.
fact: "Gone doolally" means gone mad; it is British slang.
fact: "Eccentric" is a polite word for a person who is odd or strange in a harmless way.
fact: "A real character" is a polite way to call someone odd, unusual or difficult.
fact: "Marches to the beat of a different drum" means is very unusual and does things his own way; it is American and often kind.
fact: "Lost his marbles" means gone mad or very confused.
fact: "Nutty as a fruitcake" means completely mad; it is a humorous phrase.
exercise: What does "a bit dim" mean when a British person says it about someone? | Rather stupid | Rather shy | Rather sleepy | Rather pale
exercise: Gran says the new lodger is "thick as two short planks". What does she mean? | He is very stupid | He is very strong | He is very short | He is very rude
exercise: What does "intellectually challenged" mean when said as a joke? | Stupid | Good at puzzles | Taking a hard test | Doing a brain contest
exercise: The computer man says the problem was "an ID-ten-T error". What does he really mean? | The user made a silly mistake | The computer has caught a nasty virus | A part numbered ten broke | The screen was too old
exercise: What does a teacher mean by "he's not the most academic"? | He finds school work hard | He is the oldest in class | He does not like the teacher | He is often late to school
exercise: A report says a girl is "more practical than academic". What does it mean? | She is better with her hands than at books | She is better at books than with her hands | She is very good at every subject | She prefers to play outside at break
exercise: What does "not firing on all cylinders" mean about a person? | Not thinking clearly | Not running fast | Not shooting well | Not driving a car
exercise: What does it mean if someone "has a screw loose"? | He is a bit mad | He is a good builder | His chair is broken | He lost his tools
exercise: What does "off his rocker" mean? | Mad | Off his chair | Out of a band | Fallen asleep
exercise: Mum says, "You kids are driving me round the bend." What does she mean? | You are making me crazy with annoyance | You are taking me on a drive in the car | You are making me go the wrong way round | You are making me dizzy and sick from the car ride
exercise: What does "round the twist" mean? | Mad | Dancing | Lost on a road | Very tangled
exercise: The teacher says Ella is "away with the fairies" today. What does she mean? | Ella is daydreaming and not paying attention | Ella is away on a school trip to the woods with her class | Ella is reading a fairy tale | Ella is ill and stayed home
exercise: In New Zealand, what does "off with the pixies" mean? | Daydreaming or not quite sane | Gone on a camping trip in the bush | Playing a game in the garden | Very small for one's age
exercise: What does "not all there" mean about a person? | Not quite sane or a bit slow | Not all of the family came | Late for a meeting | Missing some fingers
exercise: What does "gone doolally" mean? | Gone mad | Gone on holiday | Gone to sleep | Gone shopping
exercise: Uncle Bert wears a cape and keeps owls. People call him "eccentric". What do they mean? | He is odd in a harmless way | He is very rich and famous | He is very strict and cross | He lives in the middle of town
exercise: "Your grandpa is a real character!" What does it usually mean? | He is unusual or a bit odd | He acts in films and plays | He writes a lot of letters | He has a very strong will
exercise: What does "she marches to the beat of a different drum" mean? | She does things her own unusual way | She plays the drums in a band at school | She walks too fast for others | She is in the army marching band
exercise: What does "he's lost his marbles" mean? | He has gone mad or very confused | He has lost his toy glass balls in the park | He has lost a game | He has lost his money
exercise: What does "nutty as a fruitcake" mean? | Completely mad | Very sweet | Allergic to nuts | Very good at baking
exercise: Which of these phrases is the kindest way to say a child finds school work hard? | More practical than academic | Thick as two short planks | Nutty as a fruitcake | An ID-ten-T error
exercise: Why is it unkind to call someone who is truly mentally ill "off his rocker"? | Because it makes a joke of a real illness | Because rocking chairs are only for old people | Because the phrase is about a different country | Because it is a polite medical word
test: School report: "Tom is more practical than academic and shines in woodwork." What is the teacher really saying? | Tom finds lessons hard but is good with his hands | Tom is the cleverest pupil in every subject | Tom never comes to woodwork lessons | Tom is too practical to need any school
test: help desk note: "fixed it, ID10T error lol". what happened? | The user made a silly mistake himself | The computer had a dangerous virus | The help desk broke the computer | A cable numbered ten was missing
test: "bloody hell mate, you're thick as two short planks, it's the OTHER door." Is he talking about wood? | No, he is calling his friend very stupid | Yes, he wants two short planks | Yes, the door is made of planks | No, he is saying his friend is strong as wood
test: Sorry, I'm not firing on all cylinders today — up all night with the baby. What does she mean? | She is too tired to think clearly | Her car engine is broken | She cannot go to the shooting range | She has stopped working at the factory
test: Gossip: "Old Mr Grey's lost his marbles, he talks to his roses now." What are they saying about Mr Grey? | They think he has gone mad or confused | He has lost a bag of glass marbles in the grass | He has lost his roses from the garden | He is a famous rose grower
test: Mia reckons "eccentric" means the same as "dangerous". right? | No, it means odd in a harmless way | Yes, it is a polite word for dangerous | Yes, eccentric people are criminals | No, it means very rich and famous
test: "that new kid's a bit dim, no wait, he's just shy." In the end what does the speaker think? | The boy is shy, not stupid | The boy is stupid and shy | The boy is very bright and loud | The boy is ill and pale
test: The neighbour says her husband "marches to the beat of a different drum". Is she complaining about his music? | No, she means he does things his own unusual way | Yes, he plays the drums too loudly late at night in the shed | Yes, he joined a marching band | No, she means he walks very slowly
test: "Stop banging that pan, you're driving me round the twist!" How does the speaker feel? | Very annoyed | Very happy | Dizzy and sick | Lost and confused
test: Which of these phrases is said kindly rather than as a mocking joke? | Away with the fairies | Nutty as a fruitcake | Off his rocker | Gone doolally
test: "is my brother off with the pixies again?? he's staring at the wall." What is the brother doing? | Daydreaming and not paying attention | Playing with pixie toys | Gone out with friends | Looking for a lost pixie in the garden
test: A man calls a sick woman in hospital "not all there". Why is that unkind? | He is mocking her mind when she is unwell | He is praising her too much | He is saying she has gone home | He is using a medical word wrongly to describe her
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the expression "thick as two short planks". | Exceedingly unintelligent | Exceedingly robust | Exceedingly diminutive | Exceedingly discourteous
exam: Where a school report states that a pupil is "more practical than academic", ascertain the principal assessment conveyed. | The pupil experiences difficulty with academic work but possesses manual aptitude | The pupil excels in all academic subjects without exception | The pupil declines to undertake practical tasks of any kind | The pupil attends school on an irregular basis
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the term "eccentric" when applied to a person. | Unconventional or odd in a harmless manner | Criminal or dangerous to the public | Wealthy and of high social standing | Unwell and in need of treatment
exam: Which of the following expressions most accurately characterises a person as having become insane? | He has lost his marbles | He is not the most academic | He is a real character | He marches to a different drum
